\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Scaling directions and the hierarchical Laplacian}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{Let #{\mathcal B_k:V_k\to V_{k-1}} be specified linear maps
between real vector spaces for #{k\ge1}. A family #{R_k\in V_k},
not identically zero, is a \newvocab{scaling direction} with
positive eigenvalue #{\varepsilon} if
##{\mathcal B_kR_k=\varepsilon R_{k-1}\qquad(k\ge1).}
Relative to these maps the direction is \newvocab{relevant} when
#{\varepsilon>1}, \newvocab{marginal} when #{\varepsilon=1}, and
\newvocab{irrelevant} when #{0<\varepsilon<1}.
Here the spaces at consecutive depths can differ; the equality
compares the specified members of one family.}

\p{For operators, #{V_k} is the real span of the hierarchical
projectors on #{F_k}, and #{\mathcal B_k} is the
\vocabk{blocking step}{fdip-01CJ} #{\Phi_{\lambda,k}}.
For polynomial observables of a fixed Gaussian tower,
#{\mathcal B_k} is conditional expectation given #{T_kx=y},
as used in \ref{fdip-01CY}. These are two specified linear maps;
the latter acts on functions of the field.}

\p{With the projectors and ratio #{\lambda>0} of the blocking step,
the \newvocab{nonnegative hierarchical Laplacian} is
##{H_k=\sum_{j<k}\lambda^jP_j^{(k)}.}
Its value on constant fields is zero. In particular #{H_0=0}.
For every #{k\ge0} set #{H_k^0=\bbI}, and use the ordinary operator
powers for positive integer exponents.}
